=== QS001 profile: styleq | an underscore in a name, which is also the drop operator
some_name:1

=== clean profile: style | the same name says nothing outside the styleq profile
some_name:1

=== QS002 profile: styleq | a dot that is not a namespace
myspace.myvar:1

=== clean profile: styleq | a KDB-X module runs in its own namespace, so a dotted name is a sub-namespace
i.checkDict:{[d] d}
check:{[d] i.checkDict d}
export:([check])

=== clean profile: styleq | any assignment to export makes a module: a dictionary, or one added to (KDB-X 5, #2322)
i.helper:{x+1}
f:{i.helper x}
export:`f`g!(f;f)

=== clean profile: styleq | an export joined to, as KX's ai module writes it (#2322)
i.h:{x+1}
export:([f:{i.h x}])
export,:([g:{x*3}])

=== clean profile: styleq | an export assigned in a control statement at the top level makes a module, as KX's arrowkdb assigns it
clib.dt.printDatatype:{[x] -1 clib.dt.printDatatype_[x];}
if[5<=.z.K;export:([printDatatype:clib.dt.printDatatype])]

=== QS002 profile: styleq | an export assigned inside a lambda makes no module
myspace.myvar:1
f:{export:1; export}

=== clean profile: styleq | a leading dot is a namespace, which is the spelling the guidance asks for
.myspace.myvar:1

=== QS003 profile: styleq | `l` reads as `1`
l:1

=== QS001 QS002 QS003 QS009 profile: styleq | an assignment that is not first on its line, or opens a lambda (#662)
x:1;my_var:2
f:{l:1;l}
g:{y:1;my.var:2;y}

=== clean profile: styleq | while a name read after a `;`, an equality and a table column are no assignment (#662)
f:{[l;a] 1;l;a=1}
t:([]my_col:1 2)

=== clean profile: styleq | a name long enough to read
len:1

=== clean profile: styleq | a name being read rather than assigned is not this file's choice
r:some_other_lib_call[1]

=== QS004 profile: styleq | a single token wrapped in parentheses
